WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.320
There have to be more tombs&nbsp;
out there in need of raiding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:09.640
so why didn't 2018's "Tomb Raider" ever lead to&nbsp;
another adventure? You can thank Amazon for that.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:15.560
"Tomb Raider" was one of the hottest video game&nbsp;
series of the 1990s, centering on Lara Croft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:20.520
a relic-hunting adventurer. The first film&nbsp;
adaptation of the "Tomb Raider" franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:28.320
was Angelina Jolie's 2001 adventure flick. It&nbsp;
earned a healthy $273 million at the box office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.880
a solid take for a turn-of-the-millennium&nbsp;
action movie and enough to get a sequel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:39.040
quickly greenlit. In 2018, the baton was passed&nbsp;
to Alicia Vikander's "Tomb Raider" reboot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.760
and it earned almost the exact&nbsp;
same amount, yet there's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.760
no follow-up since. But the reason why&nbsp;
is more complicated than ticket sales.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:51.320
A sequel was in the works for a 2021&nbsp;
release, but it quickly hit a snag with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:56.040
the outbreak of COVID-19 in early 2020,&nbsp;
just as things were apparently ramping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:01.960
up. Vikander was repeatedly asked about the&nbsp;
sequel, and in a chat with Collider in 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:05.440
she seemed unsure if it was still&nbsp;
going to happen, explaining:

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:10.440
"Pre-COVID, I would've said yes. And now I think&nbsp;
it's a yes still. It's not greenlit, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:16.200
script is in the making. And I think everyone is&nbsp;
excited, and I would love to revisit Lara again."

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.000
Those delays may have cost&nbsp;
them a sequel, because in 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.120
Amazon acquired MGM, which forced the&nbsp;
franchise into a complicated legal limbo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:29.560
and the rights lapsed. An anonymous&nbsp;
studio source told The Wrap:

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:34.560
"There was a pre-negotiated ticking clock that had&nbsp;
been agreed to, and there was no way to rescue it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:40.040
in time. [MGM] asked the rights holders for&nbsp;
more time, but their patience had run out."

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:45.640
Even though "Tomb Raider" met with decidedly mixed&nbsp;
reviews upon its release, it has achieved a mild&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:50.400
cult status with fans who would love to see&nbsp;
Vikander back as Lara Croft in a second movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:55.440
And it seems the filmmakers and its cast would&nbsp;
love nothing more than to reunite for another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:02.480
go. In 2021, "Lovecraft Country" creator Misha&nbsp;
Green signed up to write and direct a sequel.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:05.120
"Howdy Tomb Raider fans. My name is Misha Green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.480
and I'm the writer and director of&nbsp;
the upcoming Tomb Raider movie.”

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:12.400
Vikander seemed particularly pumped&nbsp;
about the prospect of working with her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.160
telling Entertainment Weekly in 2021:

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:18.360
"I hope we go and make another&nbsp;
movie. Because of the pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:22.400
we had plans of shooting this film,&nbsp;
and now it's been one and a half years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.840
but Misha Green is on board, and&nbsp;
she's writing a draft right now."

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:31.880
Ever hopeful, Vikander seemed ready to strap on&nbsp;
the guns and get back to adventuring, adding:

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:35.760
"It would be so amazing if we get to go&nbsp;
and do this very big-ass film together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:39.080
going to kick some ass in front of&nbsp;
the camera and behind the camera."

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.760
A year later, though, Vikander's tone shifted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:47.080
and she cautioned that the odds were stacked&nbsp;
against them, telling MovieZine in 2022:

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:51.360
"I'm good at going back to that role, and it&nbsp;
would mean a lot of training. Now it's a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:57.200
of talk considering that Amazon has bought MGM,&nbsp;
and after the pandemic there is so much politics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.240
so right now I actually do not&nbsp;
really know what is happening."

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:04.920
If there's going to be a "Tomb Raider"&nbsp;
sequel, it would of course have to star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:10.680
Alicia Vikander. As far back as 2019, with&nbsp;
a sequel already in early development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:14.880
there wasn't actually concrete word on&nbsp;
whether Vikander would reprise the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:18.720
with People reporting that it was still&nbsp;
"unclear" whether she'd be back. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:23.000
that question was answered when the actress&nbsp;
officially signed up, and she began speaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:28.080
about the sequel publicly. Sadly, most of&nbsp;
her comments were regarding its long delay.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:32.960
Beyond Vikander, there isn't much known about&nbsp;
who else from the cast might've reappeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:38.440
in this proposed sequel, though an unverified&nbsp;
report in 2020 did suggest that Kristen Scott&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:43.040
Thomas would return and that she'd have a&nbsp;
much meatier role. The actress had played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:50.080
a smaller part as Ana Miller, Lara's mentor and a&nbsp;
colleague of her father, in the 2018 film. Ana is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:54.600
a bigger bad in the video games, and according&nbsp;
to the rumor, she would have been bumped up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:59.160
the main villain for the sequel, which had&nbsp;
been teased at the close of the first film.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.760
"You can pick up where you left off."

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:07.720
"Tomb Raider" was helmed in 2018 by a relatively&nbsp;
unknown Norwegian filmmaker named Roar Uthaug,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:11.840
who a few years later directed the cult&nbsp;
hit "Troll." The script was penned by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:17.520
Geneva Robertson-Dworet in her feature film&nbsp;
debut, alongside co-writers Evan Daugherty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:22.680
and Alastair Siddons. Robertson-Dworet went on&nbsp;
to work on "Captain Marvel," but neither she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:26.760
nor Uthaug were expected to return for&nbsp;
the second "Tomb Raider" film after it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:32.680
began development. Instead, writer Amy Jump&nbsp;
was signed in 2019 to pen the second film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:36.080
and Ben Wheatley inked a deal&nbsp;
to sit in the director's chair.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:41.280
But as the delays on the film began to pile up,&nbsp;
the studio made significant changes to the crew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:47.200
behind the camera, which saw both Amy Jump and&nbsp;
Ben Wheatley dismissed from the project. Replacing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:52.720
them was Misha Green, who was announced to&nbsp;
both write and direct the sequel in 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:57.080
though no reason was given as to the&nbsp;
swap in filmmakers. Nevertheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:02.000
Green got right to work, and by May, she was&nbsp;
telling her following on Twitter that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:07.880
first draft of the script was complete but that&nbsp;
there was "still a long journey to production."

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:11.200
"Long," in this instance, was&nbsp;
a hell of an understatement.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:14.200
"You might as well tie a stone to&nbsp;
your foot and jump off the boat now."

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:18.240
Though it took more than a year after "Tomb&nbsp;
Raider" was released before the studio was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:24.320
ready to commit to a sequel, they finally did&nbsp;
put the wheels in motion in 2019. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:29.520
rumor has it that the "Tomb Raider" sequel&nbsp;
came fairly close to beginning production. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:35.040
November 2019 report from HN Entertainment&nbsp;
claimed that Vikander was signed on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:39.800
the script was ready to go, and the studio&nbsp;
was locking down locations for shooting.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:45.960
Per that same report — which was seemingly never&nbsp;
confirmed by any of the major trades — MGM studios&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:50.280
was preparing to bring the production back&nbsp;
to the U.K., where much of the first movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:55.080
was filmed. Warner Bros.' Leavesden Studios was&nbsp;
even said to be the place where they would set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.080 --> 00:06:00.000
up shop for the sequel before it all fell apart.&nbsp;
What's known for a fact is that this version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.400
the movie was supposed to start shooting&nbsp;
in early 2020, although pandemic-related&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:08.760
shutdowns forced the project to be put on&nbsp;
hold, and then its release date was taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:13.720
off the calendar altogether. It wouldn't be&nbsp;
until 2021 that it got back up and running.

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:18.640
By then, Green was the writer and director of&nbsp;
record, and her version of the film was also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:24.200
further along than some fans may realize. As&nbsp;
it happens, it even had a title, "Tomb Raider:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:31.040
Obsidian," and Vikander was expressing excitement&nbsp;
over an imminent production start that never came.

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:34.840
Nobody but those involved know what the sequel&nbsp;
would have been about when it was under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:40.200
stewardship of writer Amy Jump and director Ben&nbsp;
Wheatley. However, some word has trickled out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:44.640
over the years of what might have happened in&nbsp;
the version written by Green, cobbled together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:49.600
from various tidbits that leaked during its&nbsp;
development and after the film was shuttered.

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:53.480
In addition to talk that Kristen Scott&nbsp;
Thomas would return in a bigger role as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:59.400
the main villain of the film, audiences also got a&nbsp;
brief glimpse of some of the action in late 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:05.960
when storyboard artist Jake Lunt Davies posted&nbsp;
a series of images that he'd created for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:09.720
project on Twitter. What they revealed&nbsp;
didn't give away too much of the plot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:13.640
but they did showcase a pulse-pounding&nbsp;
sequence that may have kicked off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:18.800
story, with a daring attack on Croft&nbsp;
Manor carried out by shadowy agents.

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:22.600
Given the ending of the 2018 "Tomb&nbsp;
Raider" — and the rumored larger role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:27.720
for Ana Williams — it's possible this raid&nbsp;
was orchestrated by Lara Croft's mentor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:32.560
sparking a quest that would take her to&nbsp;
various locations to get to the truth.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:37.360
If there's anything still standing in the way of&nbsp;
a direct sequel to 2018's "Tomb Raider," it isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:42.040
the pandemic, problems with the script,&nbsp;
or scheduling with the actors. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:46.440
it's the fact that a reboot of the&nbsp;
franchise was announced in early 2023.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:51.280
This stems from MGM's loss of the rights&nbsp;
following their acquisition by Amazon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:55.400
with an apparent bidding war taking place&nbsp;
for the chance to start from scratch.

00:07:55.400 --> 00:08:02.400
"Blockbuster news shaking Hollywood this morning.&nbsp;
Amazon buying MGM studios for 8.5 billion bucks."

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:07.280
The winners of that bidding war were of course&nbsp;
Amazon, who quickly announced grand plans for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:12.600
a total reboot of the movie with a new actor&nbsp;
taking over for Vikander. The deal was a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:17.400
one for the streaming service, which includes&nbsp;
much more than just the rights to make a feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:22.160
film. And while numbers weren't announced,&nbsp;
The Hollywood Reporter suggested it only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:26.560
fell a little short of their gargantuan deal&nbsp;
for "The Lord of The Rings." The announcement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:31.720
came on the heels of the news that Amazon would be&nbsp;
getting into the video game publishing business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:37.040
and it would be putting out the next Tomb Raider&nbsp;
console game from developer Crystal Dynamics.

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:41.960
Details at this time are still sketchy,&nbsp;
with no writer announced for the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:46.600
let alone a director or star. But that&nbsp;
doesn't mean that Amazon hasn't started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:49.360
work on the project, and they have, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:55.280
made bigger plans that could see an explosion of&nbsp;
"Tomb Raider" across multiple media platforms.

00:08:55.280 --> 00:09:00.520
After snagging the rights to a "Tomb Raider"&nbsp;
adaptation, the folks at Amazon have revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:06.000
they'll be crafting a big-screen reboot ... but&nbsp;
that isn't all. The film will be just one part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:10.720
of a larger tapestry, and they have plans&nbsp;
to turn the franchise into a full-fledged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:15.160
cinematic universe. To make that happen,&nbsp;
they have announced that a "Tomb Raider"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:19.840
TV series is in the works as a streaming&nbsp;
original, with plans for the show and film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:25.080
to be tightly integrated with both each other&nbsp;
and the already-announced video game relaunch.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:30.600
Even more exciting is that spearheading the effort&nbsp;
behind the scenes will be Phoebe Waller-Bridge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:35.040
the three-time Emmy Award-winning creator&nbsp;
and writer of "Fleabag," star of "Indiana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.920
Jones and the Dial of Destiny," and&nbsp;
co-writer of the 2021 James Bond film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:45.160
"No Time to Die." According to the trades,&nbsp;
Waller-Bridge will write the series — but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:48.960
is unlikely to be involved in the film —&nbsp;
and while many fans may have jumped to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:53.680
conclusion that she might play Lara Croft&nbsp;
too, that doesn't appear to be the case.

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:56.120
One actor who has stepped up to volunteer for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.120 --> 00:09:59.920
title role is Michaela Jae&nbsp;
Rodriguez, who told Variety:

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:04.040
"I would like to tackle a role like Lara&nbsp;
Croft. I would love to do that because I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:09.360
have the badassness and sex appeal to make people&nbsp;
love it. If not, I would also love to be Lara&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:14.840
Croft's right-hand girl who gets just as much&nbsp;
airtime and is right next to her killing it."

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:18.680
With a cinematic universe on its way that&nbsp;
will reboot the franchise with an all-new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:24.160
cast and crew, it might seem like a sequel&nbsp;
to 2018's "Tomb Raider" has no chance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:29.040
ever seeing the light of day. But if one steps&nbsp;
back and takes stock of the Hollywood landscape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:32.400
they'll see that a return to that&nbsp;
universe isn't really far-fetched,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:36.760
and it could actually still happen,&nbsp;
even if it's not anytime soon.

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:41.400
After all, recent films like "Spider-Man:&nbsp;
No Way Home" and "The Flash" have seen the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:45.520
return of actors who audiences thought&nbsp;
they'd said goodbye to decades ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:50.560
with both Tobey Maguire and Michael&nbsp;
Keaton returning as Spider-Man and Batman.

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:54.400
"...verse Angelina Jolie.&nbsp;
That would be a cool film."

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:59.160
And while there's very, very little chance that&nbsp;
Amazon would use a multiverse gimmick to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:04.640
previous Lara Crofts meet — although that's what&nbsp;
studios all seem to think the fans want — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:10.400
idea of returning to the continuity of a previous&nbsp;
movie even after a reboot isn't without precedent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:15.520
itself. The "Terminator" and "Halloween"&nbsp;
franchises both did just that, after all.

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:19.040
From the sounds of it, even star&nbsp;
Alicia Vikander would still be up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:24.120
for reprising the role years down the&nbsp;
line. Vikander told Variety in 2023:

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:28.560
"For me, that was another one of those childhood&nbsp;
dreams — to portray an action character was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:33.400
awesome and beyond what I ever thought I would do.&nbsp;
And I think the physical part is something that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:38.420
really enjoyed. So, yeah, if another opportunity&nbsp;
like that came again, I'll be interested."

